  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 226 AM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: An upper low remains centered over the Canadian \nMaritimes to our east, with high pressure building into the region \nbringing dry and seasonable conditions for the first portion of the \nweek. Temperatures today will climb into the 70s across the region \nwith some mostly sunny skies. A decaying cold front pushing through \nMonday night into early Tuesday may bring spark some light rain \nshowers, particularly near the Canadian border, although this \nfeature may lead to increased cloud cover rather than any actual \nprecipitation. Tuesday looks to be another dry and seasonably cool \nday, with highs in the upper 60s to mid 70s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: As we head into the middle of the week, a series of \nfrontal boundaries will bring chances of widespread precipitation \nfor Wednesday night into Thursday. A plume of tropical moisture will \nadvect into the region, with PWAT values in excess of 2 inches, \nallowing for a period of moderate to heavy rainfall. Fast flow aloft \nand a decent LLJ will keep the system moving, limiting the duration \nof any heavy rainfall. Additionally, limited instability and weak \nmid-level lapse rates will limit convective potential, with mostly \nbeneficial rainfall expected and any hydrological concerns unlikely. \nAt this time rainfall amounts look to generally range between 0.25 \nand 0.75 inches, although some amounts closer to an inch may be \npossible, especially with any orographic enhancement, but as we get \ncloser more detail is expected. While the bulk of precipitation is \nexpected Wednesday night into Thursday morning, a few diurnally \ndriven showers will be possible throughout the day Thursday, \nespecially across the high terrain with lingering moisture.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Following the frontal passage on Thursday, high pressure \nwill quickly establish itself across the North Country on Friday. \nPlenty of sunshine, light winds, and drier weather will ensue once \nthe high pressure system builds overhead. This will likely set up a \nclassic fog situation Friday night and Saturday night with \nwidespread dense fog across the river valleys of Vermont and \nnorthern New York. We could see some rain chances as early as \nSunday afternoon as the deterministic guidance is split on how much \na shortwave will dig out an upper level trough across the Great \nLakes region. The most likely scenario is little to no shower \nactivity on Sunday and at worst case scenario a weak front will \nbring scattered rain chances. Either way, it'll be a nice end of the \nwork week and weekend across the region. The upper level pattern \nbecomes more amplified towards the beginning of next week which \ncould potentially introduce some rain chances but if the \namplification occurs across the Rockies, it may act to enhance the \nupper level ridge and bring an extended period of dry weather to the \nNorth Country.",
